Z||000004||rogergarrison||05-16-2001||07:53 AM||||not completely sure, but i think the material is bonded to glass from factory. May have to just replace complete rear window. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87ECO5||06-15-2001||02:46 PM||||Well, it's official.  The glass window has pulled away from the convertible top on three of the four sides (top, and left/right sides).  The bottom is still attached but, because the window is able to move freely on three other sides, the top's material is beginning to tear.<P>Anyone have any suggestions?  What can I use to fasten this thing back to the material?<P>TIA||||reg||8
Z||000006||Monkeyman||06-15-2001||09:34 PM||||Gorilla glue.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000007||87ECO5||06-19-2001||09:11 AM||||I visited a local shop yesterday.  The guy took one look at it and said "about $400!"  I asked why I needed a new top.  He said the $400 was the cost of installing a new window curtain!<P>Apparently, the convertible top material will shrink over time (which mine has done - it's 9 years old) and the only way to correct the problem is to install a new curtain.<P>Needless to say, some of you living in Southern Ohio may see a red Sunbird running around town with duct tape all over the back!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000008||rogergarrison||06-19-2001||02:00 PM||||Have a clear flexible window installed. The cloth is just sewn on them. Last time I had one done (years ago) it was only $69, and that was on a Mercedes.<P>Another thing to try is JC Whitney. I think they sell just the window (plastic) just attaches with zipper at the top, fastens to bottom under the retainer strip .<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 06-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||87ECO5||06-19-2001||02:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>I think they sell just the window (plastic) just attaches with zipper at the top, fastens to bottom under the retainer strip.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>On average, how long do those plastic windows last before they begin to yellow?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000010||rogergarrison||06-19-2001||04:49 PM||||if you only wipe them with a damp cloth, they last a long time. The one on my Mercedes was done in '92 and I still see it all the time at the car wash with the guy who bought it. Still looks perfect to me.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||87ECO5||06-19-2001||07:45 PM||||WOW!  What a difference!<P>If I have the same company install a plastic window, the price drops to half!  $202 + tax (including installation) ...<P>BTW, for anyone interested, I called a local Pontiac dealership - just for s#!ts and giggles.  The plastic window curtain (from GM) for these cars has been discontinued and, according to the guy I spoke with, only two dealerships in the country have them in black (one at each dealership).  Ready for this?  $485 - JUST FOR THE PART!||||reg||10
Z||000012||rogergarrison||06-20-2001||07:57 PM||||man is that a rip off. The whole top with the rear window on the mercedes is only $457.00 at this time (parts only). and it has a finished inside headliner too.||||reg||1
